  <section class="faith-intro">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <details class="faith-intro-disclosure" data-faith-intro>
        <summary class="faith-intro-summary">
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-label" data-faith-intro-label>Read introduction</span>
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faith-intro-body">
          <p class="faith-intro-prose hero-excerpt-dropcap">The Heidelberg Catechism was written in 1563 for the churches of the Palatinate by Zacharias Ursinus and Caspar Olevianus, on commission from the elector Frederick III. It asks the reader a single, enormous question: “What is your only comfort, in life and in death?” The 128 questions that follow are an exposition of that answer - a map of the Christian life from misery through deliverance into gratitude, organized into 52 Lord’s Days for weekly preaching. Four and a half centuries later, it remains one of the warmest and most pastoral documents the Reformation produced.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
    
